Twist NEWS -- history of user-visible changes

Copyright (C) 2026 Chris Burdess

Please send Twist bug reports to <dog@gnu.org>

Version 0.1 - unreleased

  * HTTP/1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3, plus FTP, mail, MQTT, LDAP,
    WebSocket, Gopher, DICT, Telnet, RTSP, TFTP, and FILE
  * telnet sessions: the server's text is written out, its option
    negotiation is answered, and standard input (or -T) is sent; -t sets the
    terminal type, display, environment and window size
  * rtsp:// as the curl tool does it: one OPTIONS request, the reply written
    as the server sent it, exit status 85 for a CSeq that does not match;
    -I now writes the headers, and -i the blank line before the body, not
    after it
  * the cookie jar (library only) keeps every cookie for the host that set it;
    a Domain attribute widens nothing, so no Public Suffix List is needed
  * -i, -I and -D write an HTTP/1 status line with its reason phrase, as the
    server sent it, and an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 one as "HTTP/2 200", not the
    pseudo-header ":status"; -w %{http_version}; TWISTINFO_HTTP_VERSION
  * a connection is shared only between transfers with the same TLS settings
    (verification, CA, client certificate and key, an https proxy's, and
    proxy credentials)
  * Mac OS and the BSDs: the event loop no longer fails to watch a descriptor
    that is new to the kernel's queue and wanted for reading only
  * --stderr - is standard output, as curl's is; a binary body (one with a
    NUL) is not written to a terminal unless -o - says so (exit 23, and a
    warning); --no-OPTION for every option that is a switch
  * a response to HEAD, and a 204 or a 304, has no body whatever its headers
    say: -I no longer waits for one (exit 52 on HTTP/1.0, a hang on keep-alive)
  * --connect-timeout covers the greeting and the login of smtp, imap, pop3
    and ftp (and the schemes with an s), as curl's does, and not only the TCP
    connect: a server that is silent, or greets and goes quiet, ends the
    transfer at the limit with status 28
  * gzip and deflate request and response coding, via zlib
  * the progress meter and the bar (-#), and the library's
    TWISTOPT_XFERINFOFUNCTION
  * https proxies (--proxy-cacert, --proxy-insecure): TLS to the proxy and
    CONNECT in HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1, for every scheme but ftp
  * HTTP/3 through an https proxy, with CONNECT-UDP (RFC 9298), and
    --proxy-template-udp for the proxy's URI Template
  * environment variables: http_proxy, HTTPS_PROXY,
    [scheme]_PROXY, ALL_PROXY, NO_PROXY, COLUMNS, SSL_CERT_FILE, SSL_CERT_DIR,
    SSLKEYLOGFILE, QLOGDIR; with --noproxy and --capath; TWIST_CA_BUNDLE names
    a CA bundle
  * -Z/--parallel runs the URLs of a command line together, up to
    --parallel-max (50 by default, 300 at most), with curl's exit status for
    them; --fail-early ends the transfers under way, and standard input goes
    to one transfer only
  * the progress meter of -Z is one line for the run, in curl's layout for
    parallel transfers, and is drawn on standard error unless -s,
    --no-progress-meter, or a body going to a terminal says not
  * transfers started together to one host share an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3
    connection, over TLS as well as in the clear (TWISTOPT_PIPEWAIT, and
    --parallel-immediate for none), and twist_runtime_setopt with
    TWIST_RUNTIMEOPT_MAX_HOST_CONNECTIONS, or --parallel-max-host with -Z, keeps
    the connections to one host to a number
  * twist_runtime_setopt with TWIST_RUNTIMEOPT_THREADS, and --threads, run
    transfers on several event loops, each in a thread of its own but the
    first; transfers that can share a connection go to one loop, the rest are
    spread, the limit on connections to a host holds across them, and the done
    callback is always called on the thread that drives the runtime
